.TH BANDROP 1 "2026" "bandrop 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
bandrop \- secure, zero-config peer-to-peer transfer over your LAN
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bandrop send
.I path
.RI [ path ...]
.RB [ --to
.IR ip ]
.RB [ --port
.IR n ]
.RB [ --compress ]
.br
.B bandrop receive
.RB [ --port
.IR n ]
.RB [ --out
.IR dir ]
.RB [ --receipt
.IR file ]
.RB [ --overwrite ]
.RB [ --no-announce ]
.br
.B bandrop pipe
.RB { --listen | --to
.IR ip }
.RB [ --code
.IR c ]
.RB [ --port
.IR n ]
.br
.B bandrop serve
.I path
.RI [ path ...]
.RB [ --port
.IR n ]
.RB [ --once ]
.br
.B bandrop verify
.I receipt.json
.br
.B bandrop id
|
.B bandrop discover
|
.B bandrop --version
.SH DESCRIPTION
Bandrop moves data directly between machines with a short pairing code.
Traffic is end-to-end encrypted (SPAKE2 key agreement, AES-256-GCM) and never
passes through a relay or cloud service. Receivers are auto-discovered on the
local network.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B send
Send files and/or directories. Without \fB--to\fR, discovers a receiver on the
LAN. Signs a transfer receipt with your identity key.
.TP
.B receive
Wait for a transfer. \fB--receipt\fR saves a signed receipt; existing files are
auto-renamed unless \fB--overwrite\fR.
.TP
.B pipe
A secure, bidirectional stdin/stdout pipe between two machines. The connecting
side prints the code; the listening side reads it from the terminal.
.TP
.B serve
Serve files over HTTP with a URL and QR code so any browser can download them.
Plain HTTP; intended for trusted networks.
.TP
.B verify
Verify a signed receipt offline.
.TP
.B id
Print your Ed25519 identity fingerprint and public key.
.TP
.B discover
List receivers currently waiting on the LAN.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/bandrop/identity.key
Your Ed25519 private identity key (mode 0600), created on first use.
.SH AUTHOR
Ilia Banda.
